    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The rigorous logic of the **Nozickian libertarian perspective** strongly grounds the \"coercive\" argument in the **Right to Self-Ownership and Property Rights (strong natural rights theory, often Lockean in tone but more absolute in defense of property in some modern interpretations like those of Robert Nozick).\n\nFrom this viewpoint, the right to one's own person and the legitimate property derived from it are **pre-political and absolute moral claims** that the state cannot legitimately violate, regardless of the societal benefit the tax might fund.\n\nTax is seen as **unjust appropriation (or \"un-un-taxation in the best sense of the law, but in the worst sense of the right to the self in the best sense of the right to the self in the best sense of the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right to the right",
